{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-341726",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=(-7x)^{x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((-7x)^{x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-7x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-7x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =(x)^{′}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-7x))+(x)\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-7x))}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(-7x)+x\\cdot (\\frac{1}{-7x}\\cdot (-7)) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-7x)+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-7x)+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=(-7x)^{x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(-7x)^{x}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-7x)+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
